cde:TensionPneumothorax a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "tension pneumothorax" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty cde:hasEtiology ;
            owl:someValuesFrom cde:Traumatic ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty cde:hasEtiology ;
            owl:someValuesFrom cde:Iatrogenic ],
        _:N58dfce6745994c89ae8502c4b536cc52,
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty cde:scopedToSpecific ;
            owl:someValuesFrom anat:RID1363 ],
        _:Nb2fa4cd91a774ce9abd1282fb8267df7,
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty cde:hasPresence ;
            owl:someValuesFrom cde:PresenceValue ],
        _:Nc78b5817e95541d08b71ab0794e482f5,
        cde:Diagnosis ;
    skos:definition "A pneumothorax under positive pressure, displacing the mediastinum and impairing venous return. A physiological state concluded from imaging signs together with the clinical picture, not a feature seen directly." ;
    skos:exactMatch radlex:RID28525 ;
    skos:prefLabel "tension pneumothorax" ;
    cde:anchorVerdict "anchored" ;
    cde:conceptSource "Fleischner Society" ;
    cde:created "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    cde:designNote "Was briefly modelled as a present/absent DataElement on pneumothorax. That is the legacy habit of recreating presence as the name of a thing: the value list was the giveaway. No single sign establishes tension, which is what makes it a conclusion rather than an attribute." ;
    cde:hasBinding cde:binding-TensionPneumothorax-RADLEX-RID28525 ;
    cde:lastModified "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    cde:localId "DX-000027" ;
    cde:nodeType "Diagnosis" ;
    cde:sourceDefinition """Pathophysiology.—Pneumothorax refers to the presence of gas in the pleural space. Qualifiers include spontaneous, traumatic, diagnostic, and tension. Tension pneumothorax is the accumulation of intrapleural gas under pressure. In this situation, the ipsilateral lung will, if normal, collapse completely; however, a less than normally compliant lung may remain partially inflated. 
Radiographs and CT scans.—On chest radiographs, a visceral pleural edge is visible (Fig 55) unless the pneumothorax is very small or the pleural edge is not tangential to the x-ray beam. Tension pneumothorax may be associated with considerable shift of the mediastinum and/or depression of the hemidiaphragm. Some shift can occur without tension because the pleural pressure in the presence of pneumothorax becomes atmospheric, while the pleural pressure in the contralateral hemithorax remains negative.  [Fleischner Society]""" ;
    cde:versionNumber 1 ;
    cde:versionStatus "proposed" .

